ng electorate with an incumbent, Victoria Spartz, seeking another term against Democrat J. D. Ford. Spartz has out-raised Ford, though her cash on hand is modest, and national Democrats have made the suburban Indianapolis seat a target as the political environment has improved for their side. Expert raters mostly call the race Safe or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Spartz roughly an 84% chance of winning.\n\nThe only public poll, a Democratic-sponsored survey from July, showed a two-point race, which is notably closer than the district's fundamentals suggest. That survey is dated and partisan, so it warrants limited weight. The forecast lands on a Republican lead of about eight points, consistent with the seat's lean, incumbency advantage, and the weight of expert and market evidence, while acknowledging genuine Democratic interest in the race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0883, "margin": -8.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1386, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1079, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1359, "margin": -8.0}]}